<p class="lead">When researching a vehicle, you have two primary identification methods: VIN lookups and license plate lookups. While they may seem similar, they differ dramatically in what they reveal, their accuracy, legal restrictions, and usefulness for car buyers.</p>

<p>This comprehensive guide explains the critical differences between VIN and license plate lookups, helping you understand which method to use for your specific needs and why VIN lookups are the gold standard for vehicle research.</p>

<section id="overview" class="guide-section">
  <h2>VIN vs. License Plate: Quick Comparison</h2>

  <p>Before diving into details, here's a side-by-side comparison of VIN lookups versus license plate lookups:</p>

  <div class="comparison-table-wrapper">
    <table class="comparison-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Feature</th>
          <th>VIN Lookup</th>
          <th>License Plate Lookup</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Data Revealed</strong></td>
          <td>140+ manufacturer specs, safety features, recalls, engine details, assembly location</td>
          <td>Registration info, owner name (restricted), basic make/model/year</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Cost</strong></td>
          <td>Free (basic decode)</td>
          <td>Often paid or restricted</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Accuracy</strong></td>
          <td>Extremely high (federal database with validation)</td>
          <td>Varies by state (registration data can be outdated)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Privacy Restrictions</strong></td>
          <td>None (public information)</td>
          <td>Heavy restrictions (DPPA compliance required)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Permanence</strong></td>
          <td>Permanent (VIN stays with vehicle for life)</td>
          <td>Temporary (plates change with sale, registration, state)</td>
        </tr>
